    <body>
      <sec id="hht.Diagnosis">
        <title>Diagnosis</title>
        <p>Consensus clinical diagnostic criteria (Cura&#x000e7;ao criteria) for hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia (HHT) are available with proposed updates [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.shovlin.2000.66">Shovlin et al 2000</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.anderson.2022.628">Anderson et al 2022</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.mcdonald.2023.100865">McDonald et al 2023</xref>].</p>
        <sec id="hht.Suggestive_Findings">
          <title>Suggestive Findings</title>
          <p>HHT <bold>should be considered</bold> in an individual with any of the following revised Cura&#x000e7;ao criteria:</p>
          <list list-type="bullet">
            <list-item>
              <p>Recurrent nosebleeds (epistaxis). Typically &#x02265;4 per year.</p>
            </list-item>
            <list-item>
              <p>Multiple telangiectases at characteristic sites (lips, oral cavity, fingers, nose). Small blanchable red spots that are focal dilatations of post-capillary venules. Telangiectases at other sites are seen in individuals with HHT but are not specific for HHT.</p>
            </list-item>
            <list-item>
              <p>Visceral arteriovenous malformation (AVM). Typically pulmonary, cerebral, hepatic, spinal, gastrointestinal. AVMs outside these locations are uncommon and not suggestive of HHT.</p>
            </list-item>
            <list-item>
              <p>Family history. A first-degree relative in whom HHT has been diagnosed according to these criteria. Note: Any family history of the above clinical features is suggestive of HHT, but only a first-degree relative with a diagnosis of HHT based on Cura&#x000e7;ao criteria fulfills this diagnostic criterion.</p>
            </list-item>
          </list>
        </sec>
        <sec id="hht.Establishing_the_Diagnosis">
          <title>Establishing the Diagnosis</title>
          <p><bold>Clinical diagnosis.</bold> According to published consensus diagnostic criteria the clinical diagnosis of HHT can be <bold>established</bold> in a proband with <bold>three or more</bold> of the revised Cura&#x000e7;ao criteria described in <xref ref-type="sec" rid="hht.Suggestive_Findings">suggestive findings</xref>. The diagnosis of HHT is considered "possible or suspected" in an individual with <bold>two</bold> of these criteria [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.shovlin.2000.66">Shovlin et al 2000</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.anderson.2022.628">Anderson et al 2022</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.mcdonald.2023.100865">McDonald et al 2023</xref>].</p>
          <p>Note: The application of clinical diagnostic criteria to children at risk for HHT often fails to identify affected children. While signs and symptoms of HHT often develop during childhood and adolescence, epistaxis, telangiectases, and symptoms of visceral AVMs are frequently absent in affected children [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.pollak.2023.113665">Pollak et al 2023</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.mcdonald.2023.100865">McDonald et al 2023</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.iacobas.2024.592">Iacobas &#x00026; Hammill 2024</xref>] (see Management, <xref ref-type="sec" rid="hht.Evaluation_of_Relatives_at_Risk">Evaluation of Relatives at Risk</xref>).</p>
          <p><bold>Molecular diagnosis.</bold> The molecular diagnosis can be <bold>established</bold> in a proband with a germline heterozygous pathogenic (or likely pathogenic) variant in one of the genes listed in <xref ref-type="table" rid="hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta">Table 1</xref> identified by molecular genetic testing.</p>
          <p>Note: (1) Per American College of Medical Genetics and Genomics / Association for Molecular Pathology variant interpretation guidelines, the terms "pathogenic variant" and "likely pathogenic variant" are synonymous in a clinical setting, meaning that both are considered diagnostic and can be used for clinical decision making [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.richards.2015.405">Richards et al 2015</xref>]. Reference to "pathogenic variants" in this <italic toggle="yes">GeneReview</italic> is understood to include likely pathogenic variants. (2) Identification of a heterozygous variant of uncertain significance in <italic toggle="yes">ACVRL1</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">ENG, or SMAD4</italic> does not establish or rule out the diagnosis.</p>
          <p>Molecular genetic testing approaches can include a combination of <bold>gene-targeted testing</bold> (multigene panel) and <bold>comprehensive</bold>
<bold>genomic testing</bold> (exome sequencing, genome sequencing). Gene-targeted testing requires that the clinician determine which gene(s) are likely involved (see <xref ref-type="sec" rid="hht.Option_1">Option 1</xref>), whereas comprehensive genomic testing does not (see <xref ref-type="sec" rid="hht.Option_2">Option 2</xref>).</p>
          <sec id="hht.Option_1">
            <title>Option 1</title>
            <p><bold>An HHT multigene panel</bold> that includes <italic toggle="yes">ACVRL1</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">ENG</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">SMAD4</italic>, and other genes of interest (see <xref ref-type="sec" rid="hht.Differential_Diagnosis">Differential Diagnosis</xref>) is most likely to identify the genetic cause of the condition while limiting identification of pathogenic variants and variants of uncertain significance in genes that do not explain the underlying phenotype. Note: (1) The genes included in the panel and the diagnostic sensitivity of the testing used for each gene vary by laboratory and are likely to change over time. (2) Some multigene panels may include genes not associated with the condition discussed in this <italic toggle="yes">GeneReview</italic>. (3) In some laboratories, panel options may include a custom laboratory-designed panel and/or custom phenotype-focused exome analysis that includes genes specified by the clinician. (4) Methods used in a panel may include sequence analysis, deletion/duplication analysis, and/or other non-sequencing-based tests.</p>
            <p>For an introduction to multigene panels click <related-object source-id="gene" document-id="app5" object-id="app5.Multigene_Panels">here</related-object>. More detailed information for clinicians ordering genetic tests can be found <related-object source-id="gene" document-id="app5" object-id="app5.Multigene_Panels_FAQs">here</related-object>.</p>
          </sec>
          <sec id="hht.Option_2">
            <title>Option 2</title>
            <p><bold>Comprehensive</bold>
<bold>genomic testing</bold> (exome or genome sequencing) does not require the clinician to determine which gene(s) is likely involved.</p>
            <p>For an introduction to comprehensive genomic testing click <related-object source-id="gene" document-id="app5" object-id="app5.Comprehensive_Genomic_Testing">here</related-object>. More detailed information for clinicians ordering genomic testing can be found <related-object source-id="gene" document-id="app5" object-id="app5.Comprehensive_Genomic_Testing_1">here</related-object>.</p>
            <table-wrap id="hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta" orientation="portrait" position="anchor">
              <label>Table 1. </label>
              <caption>
                <p>Hereditary Hemorrhagic Telangiectasia: Molecular Genetic Testing</p>
              </caption>
              <table frame="hsides" rules="groups">
                <thead>
                  <tr>
                    <th id="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_1" rowspan="2" valign="middle" align="left" scope="rowgroup" colspan="1" headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_1">Gene&#x000a0;<sup>1,&#x000a0;2</sup></th>
                    <th id="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_2" rowspan="2" valign="middle" align="left" scope="col" colspan="1" headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_2">Proportion of HHT Attributed to Pathogenic Variants in Gene</th>
                    <th id="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" colspan="2" align="left" scope="colgroup" rowspan="1">Proportion of Pathogenic Variants&#x000a0;<sup>3</sup> Identified by Method</th>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <th headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_3" id="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_2_1" valign="middle" colspan="1" align="left" scope="colgroup" rowspan="1">Sequence analysis&#x000a0;<sup>4</sup></th>
                    <th headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_3" id="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_2_2" valign="middle" align="left" scope="col" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Gene-targeted deletion/duplication analysis&#x000a0;<sup>5</sup></th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_1" valign="middle" align="left" scope="row" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<italic toggle="yes">ACVRL1</italic>
</td>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">52%&#x000a0;<sup>6</sup></td>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_3 hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_2_1" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">90%&#x000a0;<sup>7</sup></td>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_3 hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_2_2" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">10%&#x000a0;<sup>7</sup></td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_1" valign="middle" align="left" scope="row" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<italic toggle="yes">ENG</italic>
</td>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">44%&#x000a0;<sup>6</sup></td>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_3 hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_2_1" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">90%&#x000a0;<sup>7</sup></td>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_3 hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_2_2" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">10%&#x000a0;<sup>7</sup></td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_1" valign="middle" align="left" scope="row" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<italic toggle="yes">SMAD4</italic>
</td>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1%&#x000a0;<sup>6</sup></td>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_3 hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_2_1" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003e;99%&#x000a0;<sup>7</sup></td>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_3 hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_2_2" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1 reported&#x000a0;<sup>8</sup></td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_1" valign="middle" align="left" scope="row" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Unknown</td>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">~3%&#x000a0;<sup>6,&#x000a0;9</sup></td>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_3 hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_2_1 hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_2_2" valign="middle" colspan="2" align="left" rowspan="1">NA</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
              <table-wrap-foot>
                <fn>
                  <p>NA = not applicable</p>
                </fn>
                <fn id="hht.TF.1.1">
                  <label>1. </label>
                  <p>Genes are listed in alphabetic order.</p>
                </fn>
                <fn id="hht.TF.1.2">
                  <label>2. </label>
                  <p>See <related-object source-id="gene" document-id="hht" object-id="hht.molgen.TA">Table A. Genes and Databases</related-object> for chromosome locus and protein.</p>
                </fn>
                <fn id="hht.TF.1.3">
                  <label>3. </label>
                  <p>See <xref ref-type="sec" rid="hht.Molecular_Genetics">Molecular Genetics</xref> for information on variants detected in these genes.</p>
                </fn>
                <fn id="hht.TF.1.4">
                  <label>4. </label>
                  <p>Sequence analysis detects variants that are benign, likely benign, of uncertain significance, likely pathogenic, or pathogenic. Variants may include missense, nonsense, and splice site variants and small intragenic deletions/insertions; typically, exon or whole-gene deletions/duplications are not detected. For issues to consider in interpretation of sequence analysis results, click <related-object link-type="booklink" source-id="gene" document-id="app2" document-type="book-part">here</related-object>.</p>
                </fn>
                <fn id="hht.TF.1.5">
                  <label>5. </label>
                  <p>Gene-targeted deletion/duplication analysis detects intragenic deletions or duplications. Methods used may include a range of techniques such as quantitative PCR, long-range PCR, multiplex ligation-dependent probe amplification (MLPA), and a gene-targeted microarray designed to detect single-exon deletions or duplications. Exome and genome sequencing may be able to detect deletions/duplications using breakpoint detection or read depth; however, sensitivity can be lower than gene-targeted deletion/duplication analysis.</p>
                </fn>
                <fn id="hht.TF.1.6">
                  <label>6. </label>
                  <p>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.mcdonald.2020.1963">McDonald et al [2020]</xref>
</p>
                </fn>
                <fn id="hht.TF.1.7">
                  <label>7. </label>
                  <p>Data derived from the subscription-based professional view of Human Gene Mutation Database [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.stenson.2020.1197">Stenson et al 2020</xref>]</p>
                </fn>
                <fn id="hht.TF.1.8">
                  <label>8. </label>
                  <p>To date, one large deletion has been reported in an individual with juvenile polyposis and HHT [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.wain.2014.588">Wain et al 2014</xref>].</p>
                </fn>
                <fn id="hht.TF.1.9">
                  <label>9. </label>
                  <p>Linkage analysis in one pedigree suggests a disease gene in a 7-Mb region on the short arm of chromosome 7 (7p14) (<italic toggle="yes">HHT4</italic>) [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.bayraktoydemir.2006.2155">Bayrak-Toydemir et al 2006</xref>]. Interrogation of this kindred for potentially causative variants has been performed by genomic sequencing and deletion/duplication analysis as recently as 2024 [J McDonald, unpublished data].</p>
                </fn>
              </table-wrap-foot>
            </table-wrap>
          </sec>
        </sec>
      </sec>
      <sec id="hht.Clinical_Characteristics">
        <title>Clinical Characteristics</title>
        <sec id="hht.Clinical_Description">
          <title>Clinical Description</title>
          <p>Hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia (HHT) is characterized by the presence of multiple arteriovenous malformations (AVMs) that lack intervening capillaries and result in direct connections between arteries and veins. Small AVMs are called telangiectases. The term AVM usually refers to the "large" telangiectases, greater than a few millimeters in diameter and sometimes up to several centimeters in diameter. The most common feature that brings individuals with HHT to medical attention is epistaxis (nosebleeds).</p>
          <table-wrap id="hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1" orientation="portrait" position="anchor">
            <label>Table 2. </label>
            <caption>
              <p>Hereditary Hemorrhagic Telangiectasia: Frequency of Select Features</p>
            </caption>
            <table frame="hsides" rules="groups">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th id="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_1_1" valign="middle" align="left" scope="col" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Feature</th>
                  <th id="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" align="left" scope="col" rowspan="1" colspan="1">% of Persons w/Feature&#x000a0;<sup>1</sup></th>
                  <th id="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" scope="col" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Comment</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_1_1" valign="middle" align="left" scope="row" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Epistaxis</td>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">~95%</td>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_1_1" valign="middle" align="left" scope="row" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Telangiectases</td>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">~95%</td>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Primarily on lips, tongue, face, fingers, &#x00026; buccal, nasal, &#x00026; GI mucosa</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_1_1" valign="middle" align="left" scope="row" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Anemia</td>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">50%</td>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_1_1" valign="middle" align="left" scope="row" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Pulmonary AVMs</td>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">30%-50%</td>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">More common in those w/<italic toggle="yes">ENG</italic>-related HHT</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_1_1" valign="middle" align="left" scope="row" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Hepatic AVMs</td>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">~40%-70%</td>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003c;10% are symptomatic.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_1_1" valign="middle" align="left" scope="row" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Cerebral AVMs</td>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">~10%</td>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">15%-20% if capillary telangiectases, cavernomas, &#x00026; developmental venous anomalies are included</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_1_1" valign="middle" align="left" scope="row" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Pulmonary artery hypertension</td>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">~1%-2%</td>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_1_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">WHO group 2 PH much more common</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
            <table-wrap-foot>
              <fn>
                <p>AVM = arteriovenous malformation; GI = gastrointestinal; HHT = hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia; PH = pulmonary hypertension; WHO = World Health Organization</p>
              </fn>
              <fn id="hht.TF.2.1">
                <label>1. </label>
                <p>Features of HHT are age-related.</p>
              </fn>
            </table-wrap-foot>
          </table-wrap>
          <sec id="hht.Epistaxis_Nosebleeds">
            <title>Epistaxis (Nosebleeds)</title>
            <p>Upward of 95% of individuals with HHT experience recurrent epistaxis. Epistaxis has an average age of onset of approximately 12 years [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.aassar.1991.977">Aassar et al 1991</xref>]; 33% have onset by age ten years, approximately 80% by age 20 years, and 90% before age 30 years [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.berg.2003.585">Berg et al 2003</xref>]. Nosebleeds can be either spontaneous or caused by minor trauma such as blowing, touching the nose while bathing, or dry air. Nosebleed frequency can range from several times per year to several times per day and duration can range from less than one minute up to several hours. However, many do not have nosebleeds that are frequent or severe enough to cause anemia or result in medical treatment or consultation.</p>
          </sec>
          <sec id="hht.Telangiectases">
            <title>Telangiectases</title>
            <p>Telangiectases associated with HHT are found primarily on the lips, tongue, face, fingers, and buccal, gastrointestinal (GI), and nasal mucosa. The average age of onset for non-nasal telangiectases is generally later than epistaxis but may be during childhood; 30% of affected individuals report telangiectases first appearing before age 20 years and two thirds before age 40 years [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.berg.2003.585">Berg et al 2003</xref>]. They typically appear as pinpoint- to pinhead-size macular lesions, but in older individuals are occasionally larger, raised lesions with multiple draining venules. The oral and cutaneous telangiectases associated with HHT are rarely numerous or innumerable at a given location [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.wooderchakdonahue.2019.2007">Wooderchak-Donahue et al 2019</xref>]. It is typical, even in adulthood, for an affected individual to have only ten to 20 telangiectases on careful examination [J McDonald, unpublished data].</p>
            <p>Telangiectases are distinguished from petechiae and angiomata by their ability to blanch with pressure and then quickly refill. Because of their thin walls, narrow tortuous paths, and proximity to the surface of the skin or to a mucous membrane, telangiectases can rupture and bleed after only slight trauma. Since the contractile elements in the vessel wall are lacking, given the abnormal arterial connection, bleeding from telangiectases is frequently brisk and difficult to stop. Telangiectases of the skin are significantly less likely to bleed than those of the nasal mucosa.</p>
          </sec>
          <sec id="hht.Anemia">
            <title>Anemia</title>
            <p>Epistaxis, or less commonly GI bleeding, can cause mild-to-severe iron deficiency anemia, often requiring iron replacement therapy or, less commonly, blood transfusion [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.kasthuri.2017">Kasthuri et al 2017</xref>]. GI bleeding is a more common contributor to anemia and iron deficiency in older adults. The proper terminology for description of anemia in HHT can be found in a recent consensus statement [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.alsamkari.2025b.1813">Al-Samkari et al 2025b</xref>].</p>
          </sec>
          <sec id="hht.Arteriovenous_Malformations_AVMs">
            <title>Arteriovenous Malformations (AVMs)</title>
            <p>AVMs occur most commonly in the lungs, liver, and brain. While hemorrhage is often the presenting symptom of cerebral AVMs, most lung and liver AVMs present as a consequence of blood shunting through the abnormal vessel and bypassing the capillary bed.</p>
            <p><bold>Pulmonary AVMs</bold> occur in approximately 50% of affected individuals and create high-flow right-to-left shunts [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.dupuisgirod.2017.315">Dupuis-Girod et al 2017</xref>]. Pulmonary AVMs present with a wide variety of clinical manifestations including dyspnea or hypoxemia; but even in asymptomatic individuals they pose a significant risk of serious complications including stroke, transient ischemic attacks, and brain abscess or other abscesses when air, thrombi, or bacteria shunt through the AVM, bypassing the filtering capabilities of the lungs. Less commonly, individuals with pulmonary AVMs present with hemoptysis, hemothorax, or intrapulmonary hemorrhage. Pulmonary AVMs are most frequently congenital but can enlarge with time. Migraine headache and polycythemia are additional complications of pulmonary AVMs [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.circo.2014.421">Circo &#x00026; Gossage 2014</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.meier.2018.316">Meier et al 2018</xref>].</p>
            <p>The frequency of <bold>hepatic vascular abnormalities</bold> was 74% in one study that systematically imaged the liver of affected individuals using CT [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.ianora.2004.250">Ianora et al 2004</xref>], and 41% in another study using ultrasound examination [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.buscarini.2004a.348">Buscarini et al 2004a</xref>]. However, only a minority were symptomatic (8% in those imaged by liver CT). Hepatic AVMs most often present as high-output heart failure, portal hypertension, or biliary disease [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.garciatsao.2007.499">Garcia-Tsao 2007</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.buscarini.2018.1920">Buscarini et al 2018</xref>]. Hepatic focal nodular hyperplasia is also associated with the shunting effects of blood through these high-flow vascular lesions [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.buscarini.2004b.1089">Buscarini et al 2004b</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.brenard.2010.1253">Brenard et al 2010</xref>]. HHT is generally not felt to cause cirrhosis, though imaging modalities frequently report changes suggestive of cirrhosis such as hepatic nodularity. This is more often the result of nodular regenerative hyperplasia, especially when other signs of cirrhosis are absent.</p>
            <p><bold>Cerebral AVMs</bold> are typically present at birth, occurring in approximately 10% of individuals with HHT [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.brinjikji.2017.302">Brinjikji et al 2017</xref>].</p>
            <p><bold>GI AVMs/telangiectases</bold> are common in adulthood throughout the GI mucosa, but the stomach and proximal small intestine (duodenum) are most commonly involved. Approximately one quarter of all individuals with HHT eventually have bleeding from GI telangiectases; GI bleeding is rare before age 50 years [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.plauchu.1989.291">Plauchu et al 1989</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.kjeldsen.2000.415">Kjeldsen &#x00026; Kjeldsen 2000</xref>]. It presents most often as iron deficiency anemia rather than acute GI hemorrhage, but GI bleeding is a less common cause of iron deficiency in individuals with HHT than underappreciated epistaxis. No particular foods, activities, or medications have been identified as contributors to GI bleeding in individuals with HHT.</p>
            <p><bold>Spinal AVMs</bold> appear to be significantly less common but may present with paralysis.</p>
            <p><bold>Vascular lesions in the pancreas</bold> are found in 18% of individuals with HHT but rarely result in clinical issues [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.welle.2019.2384">Welle et al 2019</xref>].</p>
          </sec>
          <sec id="hht.Pulmonary_Hypertension">
            <title>Pulmonary Hypertension</title>
            <p>Pulmonary hypertension is another pulmonary vascular manifestation of HHT, although much less common than pulmonary AVMs. Pulmonary hypertension can either result from increased cardiac output due to systemic arteriovenous shunting through liver AVMs (less commonly due to severe anemia), or be clinically and histologically indistinguishable from idiopathic pulmonary arterial hypertension [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.lyle.2016.362">Lyle et al 2016</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.revuz.2017.e0184227">Revuz et al 2017</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.vorselaars.2017b.242">Vorselaars et al 2017b</xref>] (see <related-object link-type="booklink" source-id="gene" document-id="pph" document-type="book-part">Heritable Pulmonary Arterial Hypertension Overview</related-object>).</p>
          </sec>
        </sec>
        <sec id="hht.Phenotype_Correlations_by_Gene">
          <title>Phenotype Correlations by Gene</title>
          <p>Pulmonary and cerebral AVMs are more common in individuals with <italic toggle="yes">ENG</italic> pathogenic variants, and hepatic AVMs are more common in individuals with <italic toggle="yes">ACVRL1</italic> pathogenic variants. However, pathogenic variants in both <italic toggle="yes">ENG</italic> and <italic toggle="yes">ACVRL1</italic> cause multisystem vascular dysplasias, with most manifestations associated with both [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.kjeldsen.2005.349">Kjeldsen et al 2005</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.letteboer.2006.371">Letteboer et al 2006</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.lesca.2007.14">Lesca et al 2007</xref>]. The casual HHT gene cannot be predicted with confidence based on clinical presentation [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.shovlin.2020.1907">Shovlin et al 2020</xref>].</p>
          <p>Pulmonary hypertension in the absence of severe vascular shunting, a rare HHT manifestation, occurrs most commonly in individuals with pathogenic variants in <italic toggle="yes">ACVRL1</italic> [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.vorselaars.2018.3203">Vorselaars et al 2018</xref>]<italic toggle="yes">.</italic></p>
          <p>Pathogenic variants in <italic toggle="yes">SMAD4</italic> cause a distinct syndrome with combined clinical features of <related-object link-type="booklink" source-id="gene" document-id="jps" document-type="book-part">juvenile polyposis syndrome</related-object> (JPS) and HHT, so-called JP-HHT. Historically, individuals with pathogenic variants in <italic toggle="yes">SMAD4</italic> were ascertained primarily in gastroenterology&#x000a0;/ hereditary cancer clinics based on a JPS phenotype. Reports of these JPS cohorts, in which a targeted evaluation for HHT manifestations was not typically performed, suggested that a minority had manifestations of HHT. Multiple studies in which evaluation for HHT was performed suggest that approximately 95% exhibit features of HHT, and 75%-80% meet diagnostic criteria for HHT. Thus, any pathogenic variant in <italic toggle="yes">SMAD4</italic> should be considered to cause JP-HHT with related risk for features of both disorders.</p>
          <p>There is suggestion that the prevalence of pulmonary, brain, and hepatic vascular malformations may be higher in <italic toggle="yes">SMAD4</italic>-related HHT than <italic toggle="yes">ENG-</italic> or <italic toggle="yes">ACVRL1</italic>-related HHT [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.mcdonald.2020.1963">McDonald et al 2020</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.gonzalez.2024.1">Gonzalez et al 2024</xref>]. In addition, multiple studies have shown an increased prevalence of thoracic aortopathy (31%-38%) in those with JP-HHT [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.heald.2015.1758">Heald et al 2015</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.vorselaars.2017a.114">Vorselaars et al 2017a</xref>]. One study found two thirds of individuals with JP-HHT to have some manifestation of a connective tissue disorder, mostly joint laxity, followed by micrognathia and thoracic deformities [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.gonzalez.2024.1">Gonzalez et al 2024</xref>].</p>
        </sec>
        <sec id="hht.GenotypePhenotype_Correlations">
          <title>Genotype-Phenotype Correlations</title>
          <p>Essentially all known HHT pathogenic variants are null alleles, and no absolute genotype-phenotype correlations exist between clinical phenotypes and specific pathogenic variants or types of variants [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.mcdonald.2015.1">McDonald et al 2015</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.shovlin.2020.1907">Shovlin et al 2020</xref>].</p>
        </sec>
        <sec id="hht.Penetrance">
          <title>Penetrance</title>
          <p>The average age of onset is late childhood for epistaxis and early adulthood for mucocutaneous telangiectases. HTT is near fully penetrant by late adulthood [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.faughnan.2011.73">Faughnan et al 2011</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.mcdonald.2023.100865">McDonald et al 2023</xref>].</p>
        </sec>
        <sec id="hht.Nomenclature">
          <title>Nomenclature</title>
          <p>The original description of the syndrome was made by Sutton in 1864. However, Osler in the United States, Parkes Weber in the United Kingdom, and Rendu in France are typically given credit; hence the eponymous Osler-Weber-Rendu syndrome (or Rendu-Osler-Weber in articles in the French literature). The designation hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia was suggested in 1912 by one of Osler's residents.</p>
          <p>The gene now identified as <italic toggle="yes">ACVRL1</italic> is referred to in many publications as <italic toggle="yes">ALK1</italic>. Note: Other genes were also previously named <italic toggle="yes">ALK1</italic>.</p>
        </sec>
        <sec id="hht.Prevalence">
          <title>Prevalence</title>
          <p>The estimated prevalence based on clinically diagnosed individuals is approximately one in 5,000 [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.faughnan.2020.989">Faughnan et al 2020</xref>]. Yet the consensus of HHT medical experts has been that the true prevalence is considerably higher than suggested by reported individuals. A study that estimated prevalence from gnomAD allele frequencies of predicted pathogenic variants in <italic toggle="yes">ENG</italic> and <italic toggle="yes">ACVRL1</italic> calculated a prevalence of between 2.1 in 5,000 and 11.9 in 5,000 [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.anzell.2025.e005061">Anzell et al 2025</xref>].</p>
          <p>HHT occurs with wide ethnic and geographic distribution. A recent systematic literature review of reported founder effects identified 26 <italic toggle="yes">ACVRL1</italic> and 12 <italic toggle="yes">ENG</italic> variants with potential founder effects. Ain, Jura, and Deux-Sevres departments (regions) of France (<italic toggle="yes">ACVRL1</italic> pathogenic variant) and the islands of Cura&#x000e7;ao and Bonaire in the Netherlands Antilles (<italic toggle="yes">ENG</italic> pathogenic variant) are among the regions with identified founder variants [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.major.2021.1682">Major et al 2021</xref>].</p>
        </sec>
      </sec>
      <sec id="hht.Genetically_Related_Allelic_Disorder">
        <title>Genetically Related (Allelic) Disorders</title>
        <p>No phenotypes other than those discussed in this <italic toggle="yes">GeneReview</italic> are known to be associated with germline pathogenic variants in <italic toggle="yes">ACVRL1</italic> or <italic toggle="yes">ENG</italic>.</p>
        <p><bold><italic toggle="yes">SMAD4.</italic></bold><related-object link-type="booklink" source-id="gene" document-id="myhre" document-type="book-part"> Myhre syndrome</related-object>, a multisystem progressive connective tissue disorder, is associated with heterozygous pathogenic variants in <italic toggle="yes">SMAD4.</italic> Unlike hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia (which is caused by heterozygous loss-of-function pathogenic variants), studies have suggested a gain-of-function or dominant-negative mechanism of disease causation for <italic toggle="yes">SMAD4</italic> pathogenic variants associated with Myhre syndrome.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="hht.Differential_Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p><bold>Recurrent epistaxis</bold> can be a sign of various bleeding diatheses including <related-object link-type="booklink" source-id="gene" document-id="von-willebrand" document-type="book-part">von Willebrand disease</related-object> (see <xref ref-type="table" rid="hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_2">Table 3</xref>), but may also be seen in otherwise healthy individuals.</p>
        <p><bold>Telangiectases</bold> occur in a number of conditions:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Hereditary disorders including those of known genetic cause (see <xref ref-type="table" rid="hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_2">Table 3</xref>) and unknown genetic cause:</p>
            <list list-type="bullet">
              <list-item>
                <p>CREST (<italic toggle="yes">c</italic>alcinosis, <italic toggle="yes">R</italic>aynaud phenomenon, <italic toggle="yes">e</italic>sophageal dysmotility, <italic toggle="yes">s</italic>clerodactyly, <italic toggle="yes">t</italic>elangiectasia) syndrome [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.gilligan.2024.1368">Gilligan et al 2024</xref>]</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p>Hereditary benign telangiectasia (OMIM <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://omim.org/entry/187260">187260</ext-link>). Characterized by widespread telangiectases, predominantly on the face, upper limbs, and upper trunk. The telangiectases are venular and associated with upper dermal atrophy. It should be suspected in persons without a history or family history of nosebleed or other bleeding and no mucosal telangiectases, arteriovenous malformations (AVMs), or characteristic pattern of telangiectasia distribution found in hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia (HHT).</p>
              </list-item>
            </list>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Pregnancy. These can appear similar to the telangiectases of HHT but often regress after pregnancy has ended.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Chronic liver disease. (However, telangiectases associated with chronic liver disease are almost always of the "spider" class and occur on the face and chest and around the umbilicus.)</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Telangiectases may also be seen in otherwise healthy individuals. Adults may develop one or a couple of cutaneous telangiectases with age. These often raise concern, especially when the person also has multiple cherry angiomata, which are quite frequent in older individuals and are unrelated to HHT.</p>
        <p><bold>AVMs</bold> occur in other vascular dysplasia syndromes (see <xref ref-type="table" rid="hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_2">Table 3</xref>). The absence of telangiectases specifically on the lips, oral cavity, and fingers best distinguish other vascular dysplasias from HHT.</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Pulmonary AVMs.</bold> The majority of individuals (80%-95%) with a pulmonary AVM have HHT [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.wong.2011.368">Wong et al 2011</xref>]. A significant minority of isolated pulmonary AVMs represent sporadic events.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Cerebral AVMs</bold> occur most frequently as an isolated finding but may be a manifestation of HHT or another dominantly inherited vascular dysplasia. Families have also been reported with autosomal dominant AVMs of the brain and no other features of HHT. However, the presence of multiple cerebral AVMs is highly suggestive of HHT [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.bharatha.2012.72">Bharatha et al 2012</xref>].</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p><bold>Hereditary disorders of known genetic</bold>
<bold>cause.</bold>
<xref ref-type="table" rid="hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_2">Table 3</xref> lists disorders of known genetic cause in the differential diagnosis of HHT. Of these disorders, <italic toggle="yes">EPHB4</italic>-related capillary malformation-arteriovenous malformation syndrome (CM-AVM) is the most often mistaken for HHT. <italic toggle="yes">RASA</italic>-related CM-AVM, <italic toggle="yes">GDF2</italic>-related vascular anomaly, and <italic toggle="yes">BMPR2</italic>-related disorder are less commonly confused, but share some phenotypic overlap with HHT. A multigene panel that includes all genes known to be associated with HHT, CM-AVM, and <italic toggle="yes">BMPR2</italic> is indicated when HHT is suspected. (Note: Molecular genetic testing is not necessary to distinguish between HHT, ataxia-telangiectasia, and von Willebrand disease.)</p>
        <table-wrap id="hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_2" orientation="portrait" position="anchor">
          <label>Table 3. </label>
          <caption>
            <p>Hereditary Hemorrhagic Telangiectasia: Genetic Differential Diagnosis</p>
          </caption>
          <table frame="hsides" rules="groups">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th id="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_2_1_1_1_1" valign="middle" align="left" scope="col" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Gene(s)</th>
                <th id="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_2_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" align="left" scope="col" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Disorder/<break/>Phenotype</th>
                <th id="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_2_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" scope="col" rowspan="1" colspan="1">MOI</th>
                <th id="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_2_1_1_1_4" valign="middle" align="left" scope="col" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Key Feature(s) Similar to HHT</th>
                <th id="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_2_1_1_1_5" valign="middle" align="left" scope="col" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Comment</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_2_1_1_1_1" valign="middle" align="left" scope="row" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<italic toggle="yes">ATM</italic>
</td>
                <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_2_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><related-object link-type="booklink" source-id="gene" document-id="ataxia-telangiectas" document-type="book-part">Ataxia-telangiectasia</related-object><break/>(A-T)</td>
                <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_2_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">AR</td>
                <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_2_1_1_1_4" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Telangiectases</td>
                <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_2_1_1_1_5" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Telangiectases occur in the conjunctiva &#x00026; sun-exposed skin (e.g., ears, face, neck, hands), &#x00026; are usually evident by age 6 yrs, but not at disease onset.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Classic A-T is characterized by childhood onset of progressive neurologic manifestations. immunodeficiency, pulmonary disease, &#x00026; increased risk of malignancy. In most children w/classic A-T, cerebellar ataxia is the presenting manifestation observed after the child starts to sit &#x00026; walk.</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_2_1_1_1_1" valign="middle" align="left" scope="row" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<italic toggle="yes">BMPR2</italic>
</td>
                <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_2_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><italic toggle="yes">BMPR2</italic>-related disorder</td>
                <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_2_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">AD</td>
                <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_2_1_1_1_4" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>PAH</p></list-item><list-item><p>Pulmonary AVMs</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_2_1_1_1_5" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>PAH is the common presentation (see <related-object link-type="booklink" source-id="gene" document-id="pph" document-type="book-part">Heritable Pulmonary Arterial Hypertension Overview</related-object>).</p></list-item><list-item><p>Rare cause of pulmonary AVM (8 persons reported)</p></list-item><list-item><p>5/8 w/multiple pulmonary AVM. 3/8 reported epistaxis, 1/8 w/unspecified "mucocutaneous" telangiectases, 3/8 w/no PAH.&#x000a0;<sup>1</sup></p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_2_1_1_1_1" valign="middle" align="left" scope="row" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<italic toggle="yes">EPHB4</italic>
<break/>
<italic toggle="yes">RASA1</italic>
</td>
                <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_2_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<related-object link-type="booklink" source-id="gene" document-id="rasa1-rel-dis" document-type="book-part">Capillary malformation-arteriovenous malformation syndrome</related-object>
</td>
                <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_2_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">AD</td>
                <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_2_1_1_1_4" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Cerebral AVMs</p></list-item><list-item><p>Epistaxis</p></list-item><list-item><p>Telangiectases</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_2_1_1_1_5" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Characterized by multiple, small (1-2 cm in diameter) CMs mostly on face &#x00026; limbs</p></list-item><list-item><p>May be assoc w/cerebral, spinal, hepatic, facial, &#x00026; extremity AVMs &#x00026; arteriovenous fistulas&#x000a0;<sup>2</sup></p></list-item><list-item><p>Telangiectases occur but are less common dermal lesions than CM. Reported primarily in <italic toggle="yes">EPHB4-</italic>related CM-AVM; typically on perioral region, lips, upper trunk, &#x00026; arms/legs. Unlike HHT, telangiectases are usually "numerous" or "innumerable" at an involved site &#x00026; are often surrounded by a white halo. Except for lips, commonly affected areas differ from those in HHT.&#x000a0;<sup>3</sup></p></list-item><list-item><p>Cutaneous telangiectases are less frequently reported in <italic toggle="yes">RASA1</italic>-related CM-AVM, but 3 persons are reported to have telangiectases in locations characteristic of HHT; 2 also had epistaxis, 1 had pulmonary AVM, &#x00026; 2 had liver vascular malformations typical for HHT.&#x000a0;<sup>4</sup></p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_2_1_1_1_1" rowspan="2" valign="middle" align="left" scope="rowgroup" colspan="1">
<italic toggle="yes">GDF2</italic>
</td>
                <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_2_1_1_1_2" rowspan="2" valign="middle" align="left" colspan="1"><italic toggle="yes">GDF2</italic>-related vascular anomaly syndrome</td>
                <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_2_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">AD</td>
                <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_2_1_1_1_4" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Cerebral AVMs</p></list-item><list-item><p>Telangiectases</p></list-item><list-item><p>Epistaxis</p></list-item><list-item><p>PAH</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_2_1_1_1_5" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Isolated PAH is the most common presentation.&#x000a0;<sup>5</sup></p></list-item><list-item><p>A small number of persons have been reported w/1 or 2 of the following: pulmonary AVM, cerebral AVM, epistaxis, or telangiectases. Most telangiectases, when described, are atypical for HHT.&#x000a0;<sup>6</sup></p></list-item><list-item><p>Only 2 reported persons met clinical diagnostic criteria for HHT.&#x000a0;<sup>7</sup></p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_2_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" colspan="1" align="left" scope="row" rowspan="1">AR</td>
                <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_2_1_1_1_4" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>PAH</p></list-item><list-item><p>Telangiectases</p></list-item><list-item><p>Pulmonary AVMs</p></list-item><list-item><p>Epistaxis</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_2_1_1_1_5" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Homozygous pathogenic variants in <italic toggle="yes">GDF2</italic> have been reported in 3 persons: 1 w/PAH &#x00026; linear facial telangiectases, 1 w/multiple pulmonary AVMs, 1 w/diffuse small/microscopic pulmonary AVMs &#x00026; epistaxis.&#x000a0;<sup>8</sup></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_2_1_1_1_1" valign="middle" align="left" scope="row" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<italic toggle="yes">VWF</italic>
</td>
                <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_2_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<related-object link-type="booklink" source-id="gene" document-id="von-willebrand" document-type="book-part">Von Willebrand disease</related-object>
</td>
                <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_2_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">AD<break/>AR</td>
                <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_2_1_1_1_4" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Recurrent epistaxis</td>
                <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_2_1_1_1_5" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Other bleeding diatheses can also be considered.</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <table-wrap-foot>
            <fn>
              <p>AD = autosomal dominant; AR = autosomal recessive; A-T = ataxia-telangiectasia; AVM = arteriovenous malformation; CM = capillary malformation; CM-AVM = capillary malformation-arteriovenous malformation syndrome; HHT = hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia; GI = gastrointestinal; MOI = mode of inheritance; PAH = pulmonary arterial hypertension</p>
            </fn>
            <fn id="hht.TF.3.1">
              <label>1. </label>
              <p><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.rigelsky.2008.2551">Rigelsky et al [2008]</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.handa.2014.195">Handa et al [2014]</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.soon.2014.1435">Soon et al [2014]</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.arano.2021.101352">Arano et al [2021]</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.chuang.2022.542">Chuang et al [2022]</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.scarpato.2023.e201">Scarpato et al [2023]</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.kularatne.2023.e23">Kularatne et al [2023]</xref></p>
            </fn>
            <fn id="hht.TF.3.2">
              <label>2. </label>
              <p>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.guilhem.2023.905">Guilhem et al [2023]</xref>
</p>
            </fn>
            <fn id="hht.TF.3.3">
              <label>3. </label>
              <p>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.wooderchakdonahue.2019.2007">Wooderchak-Donahue et al [2019]</xref>
</p>
            </fn>
            <fn id="hht.TF.3.4">
              <label>4. </label>
              <p><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.hernandez.2015.15040">Hernandez et al [2015]</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.el_hajjam.2021.645">El Hajjam et al [2021]</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.guilhem.2023.905">Guilhem et al [2023]</xref></p>
            </fn>
            <fn id="hht.TF.3.5">
              <label>5. </label>
              <p><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.gr_f.2018.1416">Gr&#x000e4;f et al [2018]</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.hodgson.2020.575">Hodgson et al [2020]</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.grynblat.2024.2301634">Grynblat et al [2024]</xref></p>
            </fn>
            <fn id="hht.TF.3.6">
              <label>6. </label>
              <p>It is uncertain whether <italic toggle="yes">GDF2</italic>-related vascular anomaly syndrome will ultimately be considered a type of HHT versus a rarer overlapping but distinct disorder [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.wooderchakdonahue.2013.530">Wooderchak-Donahue et al 2013</xref>]. However, cases reported, including three individuals with pulmonary AVM and two individuals with cerebral AVM, suggest that medical management similar to that recommended for individuals with <italic toggle="yes">ACVRL1-</italic> or <italic toggle="yes">ENG-</italic>related HHT is indicated [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.hernandez.2015.15040">Hernandez et al 2015</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.topiwala.2020.105301">Topiwala et al 2020</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.balachandar.2022.959">Balachandar et al 2022</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.farhan.2022.199">Farhan et al 2022</xref>,<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.oliver.2025.3359">Oliver et al 2025</xref>].</p>
            </fn>
            <fn id="hht.TF.3.7">
              <label>7. </label>
              <p>One <italic toggle="yes">GDF2</italic> variant of uncertain significance and one pathogenic variant were identified in individuals who met clinical criteria for HHT [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.hernandez.2015.15040">Hernandez et al 2015</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.balachandar.2022.959">Balachandar et al 2022</xref>].</p>
            </fn>
            <fn id="hht.TF.3.8">
              <label>8. </label>
              <p><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.liu.2020.e20191970">Liu et al [2020]</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.hodgson.2021.e1685">Hodgson et al [2021]</xref></p>
            </fn>
          </table-wrap-foot>
        </table-wrap>
      </sec>
      <sec id="hht.Management">
        <title>Management</title>
        <p>Guidelines for management of hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia (HHT) have been published [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.faughnan.2011.73">Faughnan et al 2011</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.faughnan.2020.989">Faughnan et al 2020</xref>]. They should be considered supplementary to those recommended by an expert in clinical management of HHT as typically seen at HHT Centers of Excellence.</p>
        <sec id="hht.Evaluations_Following_Initial_Diagno">
          <title>Evaluations Following Initial Diagnosis</title>
          <p>To establish the extent of disease and needs in an individual diagnosed with HHT, the evaluations summarized in <xref ref-type="table" rid="hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_3">Table 4</xref> (if not performed as part of the evaluation that led to the diagnosis) are recommended [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.faughnan.2011.73">Faughnan et al 2011</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.hosman.2017.1206">Hosman et al 2017</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.faughnan.2020.989">Faughnan et al 2020</xref>].</p>
          <table-wrap id="hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_3" orientation="portrait" position="anchor">
            <label>Table 4. </label>
            <caption>
              <p>Hereditary Hemorrhagic Telangiectasia: Recommended Evaluations Following Initial Diagnosis</p>
            </caption>
            <table frame="hsides" rules="groups">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th id="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_3_1_1_1_1" valign="middle" align="left" scope="col" rowspan="1" colspan="1">System/Concern</th>
                  <th id="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_3_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" align="left" scope="col" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Evaluation</th>
                  <th id="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_3_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" scope="col" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Comment</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_3_1_1_1_1" rowspan="3" valign="middle" align="left" scope="rowgroup" colspan="1">
<bold>Epistaxis&#x000a0;/</bold>
<break/>
<bold>Other bleeding&#x000a0;/</bold>
<break/>
<bold>Anemia</bold>
</td>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_3_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Assess for history of epistaxis &#x00026; GI bleeding.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Nasal, oral, &#x00026; cutaneous exam for telangiectases</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_3_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Also consider other causes of anemia if disproportionate to the amount of epistaxis; medical problems unrelated to HHT (e.g., ulcers, colon cancer) can cause GI blood loss.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_3_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" colspan="1" align="left" scope="row" rowspan="1">Complete blood count to assess for anemia &#x00026;/or polycythemia</td>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_3_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Polycythemia raises suspicion for large pulmonary AVMs.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_3_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" colspan="1" align="left" scope="col" rowspan="1">Ferritin level to assess for iron deficiency</td>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_3_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_3_1_1_1_1" valign="middle" align="left" scope="row" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Pulmonary AVMs&#x000a0;/</bold>
<break/>
<bold>Pulmonary arterial</bold>
<break/>
<bold>hypertension</bold>
</td>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_3_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Assess for history of TIA, stroke, dyspnea, migraines, or hemoptysis.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Exam for clubbing &#x00026; cyanosis</p></list-item><list-item><p>In those diagnosed in <bold>adulthood</bold>: TCE w/agitated saline contrast for detection of pulmonary shunting&#x000a0;/ AVMs, w/estimation of pulmonary artery systolic pressure</p></list-item><list-item><p>In those diagnosed in <bold>childhood</bold>: TCE w/agitated saline contrast <bold>OR</bold> chest radiograph w/pulse oximetry; if chest radiograph is abnormal or oxygen saturation is &#x0003c;96%, proceed w/TCE w/agitated saline contrast.</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_3_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>A history of stroke is seen in approximately 30% of individuals but clubbing &#x00026; cyanosis are uncommon in the current era.</p></list-item><list-item><p>When pulmonary shunting is suggested by TCE, perform CT chest w/ or w/o contrast to look for treatable pulmonary AVM. Some HHT centers only recommend CT if TCE shows more than mild shunt.&#x000a0;<sup>1</sup></p></list-item><list-item><p>Most serious complications of pulmonary AVM during childhood have occurred in hypoxemic children w/saturation &#x0003c;93%-96%.</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_3_1_1_1_1" valign="middle" align="left" scope="row" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Hepatic AVMs</bold>
</td>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_3_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>History &#x00026; exam for signs of heart failure or liver failure</p></list-item><list-item><p>Imaging options in adults include Doppler ultrasound (best option when local expertise is available), multiphase CT angiography, &#x00026; contrast abdominal MRI.</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_3_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">The latest guidelines recommend imaging in adults to screen for hepatic AVMs.&#x000a0;<sup>2</sup> Although they are usually asymptomatic, it is felt that early detection of hepatic AVM will allow for better prognostication &#x00026; follow up. The optimal age to start screening is unknown.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_3_1_1_1_1" valign="middle" align="left" scope="row" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Cerebral AVMs</bold>
</td>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_3_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Head MRI (w/ &#x00026; w/o contrast using sequences that detect blood products) to assess for AVMs</td>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_3_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>As early as possible, preferably in 1st yr of life</p></list-item><list-item><p>Cerebral AVM can cause life-threatening cerebral hemorrhage before age 12 mos.</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_3_1_1_1_1" valign="middle" align="left" scope="row" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold><italic toggle="yes">SMAD4</italic>-related HHT</bold>
</td>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_3_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">See Juvenile Polyposis Syndrome, <related-object source-id="gene" document-id="jps" object-id="jps.Evaluations_Following_Initial_Diagno">Evaluations Following Initial Diagnosis</related-object> for additional recommendations.</td>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_3_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Colonoscopy at age 15 yrs; repeat every 3 yrs if no polyps found, or annually w/EGD if colonic polyps are identified.&#x000a0;<sup>2</sup></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_3_1_1_1_1" valign="middle" align="left" scope="row" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Genetic</bold>
<break/>
<bold>counseling</bold>
</td>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_3_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">By genetics professionals&#x000a0;<sup>3</sup></td>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_3_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">To obtain a pedigree &#x00026; inform affected persons &#x00026; their families re nature, MOI, &#x00026; implications of HHT to facilitate medical &#x00026; personal decision making</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
            <table-wrap-foot>
              <fn>
                <p>AVMs = arteriovenous malformations; EGD = esophagogastroduodenoscopy; GI = gastrointestinal; HHT = hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia; MOI = mode of inheritance; TCE = transthoracic contrast echocardiography; TIA = transient ischemic attack</p>
              </fn>
              <fn id="hht.TF.4.1">
                <label>1. </label>
                <p>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.velthuis.2015.255">Velthuis et al [2015]</xref>
</p>
              </fn>
              <fn id="hht.TF.4.2">
                <label>2. </label>
                <p>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.faughnan.2020.989">Faughnan et al [2020]</xref>
</p>
              </fn>
              <fn id="hht.TF.4.3">
                <label>3. </label>
                <p>Clinical geneticist, certified genetic counselor, certified genetic nurse, genetics advanced practice provider (nurse practitioner or physician assistant)</p>
              </fn>
            </table-wrap-foot>
          </table-wrap>
        </sec>
        <sec id="hht.Treatment_of_Manifestations">
          <title>Treatment of Manifestations</title>
          <sec id="hht.Targeted_Therapies">
            <title>Targeted Therapies</title>
            <p content-type="targeted_therapy">
<italic toggle="yes">In GeneReviews, a targeted therapy is one that addresses the specific underlying mechanism of disease causation (regardless of whether the therapy is significantly efficacious for one or more manifestation of the genetic condition); would otherwise not be considered without knowledge of the underlying genetic cause of the condition; or could lead to a cure</italic>. &#x02014;ED</p>
            <table-wrap id="hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_4" orientation="portrait" position="anchor">
              <label>Table 5. </label>
              <caption>
                <p>Hereditary Hemorrhagic Telangiectasia: Targeted Therapies</p>
              </caption>
              <table frame="hsides" rules="groups">
                <thead>
                  <tr>
                    <th id="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_4_1_1_1_1" valign="middle" align="left" scope="col" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Type</th>
                    <th id="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_4_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" align="left" scope="col" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Treatment</th>
                    <th id="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_4_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" scope="col" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Dosage</th>
                    <th id="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_4_1_1_1_4" valign="middle" align="left" scope="col" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Consideration</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_4_1_1_1_1" valign="middle" align="left" scope="row" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Antifibrinolytics</td>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_4_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Tranexamic acid</td>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_4_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Children: dose per HHT expert</p></list-item><list-item><p>Adults: start at 500 mg 2x/day, gradually increasing up to 1,000 mg 4x/day OR 1,500 mg 3x/day&#x000a0;<sup>1</sup></p></list-item><list-item><p>Note: (1) Dose should be reviewed by HHT expert. (2) A 650-mg tablet is available in the US &#x00026; a common dose is 1,300 mg 3x/day.</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_4_1_1_1_4" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Off-label use;&#x000a0;<sup>2</sup> recommended for HHT-related epistaxis &#x00026; GI bleeding</p></list-item><list-item><p>Supported by 2 RCTs &#x00026; several uncontrolled trials showing reduced epistaxis&#x000a0;<sup>3</sup></p></list-item><list-item><p>Although there is a theoretical risk of thrombosis, this has been a rare complication in those w/HHT.</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_4_1_1_1_1" rowspan="3" valign="middle" align="left" scope="rowgroup" colspan="1">Antiangiogenics</td>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_4_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Bevacizumab</td>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_4_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>5 mg/kg IV every 2 weeks for 4-6 doses, then every 1-6 months as needed</p></list-item><list-item><p>Note: Dose should be reviewed by HHT expert.&#x000a0;<sup>4</sup></p></list-item></list>
</td>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_4_1_1_1_4" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Off label use;&#x000a0;<sup>2</sup> recommended for HHT-related epistaxis &#x00026; GI bleeding</p></list-item><list-item><p>Supported by multiple retrospective studies showing reduced epistaxis, increased hemoglobin, &#x00026; reduced RBC transfusion need&#x000a0;<sup>5</sup></p></list-item></list>
</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_4_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" colspan="1" align="left" scope="row" rowspan="1">Pomalidomide</td>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_4_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>4 mg daily</p></list-item><list-item><p>Note: Dose should be reviewed by HHT expert.&#x000a0;<sup>4</sup></p></list-item></list>
</td>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_4_1_1_1_4" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Off label use;&#x000a0;<sup>2</sup> recommended for refractory HHT-related epistaxis &#x00026; GI bleeding</p></list-item><list-item><p>Supported by 1 RCT showing reduced epistaxis severity &#x00026; improved QOL&#x000a0;<sup>6</sup></p></list-item></list>
</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_4_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" colspan="1" align="left" scope="row" rowspan="1">Pazopanib</td>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_4_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>50-200 mg daily</p></list-item><list-item><p>Note: Dose should be reviewed by HHT expert.&#x000a0;<sup>4</sup></p></list-item></list>
</td>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_4_1_1_1_4" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Off label use;&#x000a0;<sup>2</sup> recommended for refractory HHT-related epistaxis &#x00026; GI bleeding</p></list-item><list-item><p>Supported by several retrospective &#x00026; prospective studies showing reduced epistaxis &#x00026; improved Hgb&#x000a0;<sup>7</sup></p></list-item><list-item><p>A placebo-controlled RCT is currently under way w/results expected 2026.</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
              <table-wrap-foot>
                <fn>
                  <p>GI = gastrointestinal; Hgb = hemoglobin; HHT = hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia; IV = intravenous; QOL = quality of life; RBC = red blood cell; RCT = randomized clinical trial; US = United States</p>
                </fn>
                <fn id="hht.TF.5.1">
                  <label>1. </label>
                  <p><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.faughnan.2020.989">Faughnan et al [2020]</xref>, supplement</p>
                </fn>
                <fn id="hht.TF.5.2">
                  <label>2. </label>
                  <p>To date, no targeted therapies are FDA approved specifically for use in individuals with HHT.</p>
                </fn>
                <fn id="hht.TF.5.3">
                  <label>3. </label>
                  <p><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.gaillard.2014.1494">Gaillard et al [2014]</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.geisthoff.2014.565">Geisthoff et al [2014]</xref></p>
                </fn>
                <fn id="hht.TF.5.4">
                  <label>4. </label>
                  <p>To date, use of bevacizumab, pomalidomide, and pazopanib in the pediatric population is limited and recommended dosage is not available in this population.</p>
                </fn>
                <fn id="hht.TF.5.5">
                  <label>5. </label>
                  <p><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.alsamkari.2021.2161">Al-Samkari et al [2021]</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.alsamkari.2022.514">Al-Samkari [2022]</xref></p>
                </fn>
                <fn id="hht.TF.5.6">
                  <label>6. </label>
                  <p>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.alsamkari.2024.1015">Al-Samkari et al [2024]</xref>
</p>
                </fn>
                <fn id="hht.TF.5.7">
                  <label>7. </label>
                  <p><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.faughnan.2019.145">Faughnan et al [2019]</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.parambil.2022.87">Parambil et al [2022]</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.lewandowska.2025.525">Lewandowska et al [2025]</xref></p>
                </fn>
              </table-wrap-foot>
            </table-wrap>
          </sec>
          <sec id="hht.Supportive_Care">
            <title>Supportive Care</title>
            <p>Supportive care to improve quality of life, maximize function, and reduce complications is recommended. This ideally involves multidisciplinary care by specialists in relevant fields (see <xref ref-type="table" rid="hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_5">Table 6</xref>).</p>
            <table-wrap id="hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_5" orientation="portrait" position="anchor">
              <label>Table 6. </label>
              <caption>
                <p>Hereditary Hemorrhagic Telangiectasia: Treatment of Manifestations</p>
              </caption>
              <table frame="hsides" rules="groups">
                <thead>
                  <tr>
                    <th id="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_5_1_1_1_1" valign="middle" align="left" scope="col" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Manifestation/<break/>Concern</th>
                    <th id="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_5_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" align="left" scope="col" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Treatment</th>
                    <th id="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_5_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" scope="col" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Considerations/Other</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_5_1_1_1_1" rowspan="3" valign="middle" align="left" scope="rowgroup" colspan="1">
<bold>Nosebleeds</bold>
<break/>
<bold>(epistaxis)</bold>
</td>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_5_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Humidification</p></list-item><list-item><p>2x daily topical moisturizing therapy</p></list-item><list-item><p>Hemostatic products (e.g., gauze, sponge, powder products)</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_5_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Consider additional treatments if epistaxis is causing anemia or interfering w/normal activities.</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_5_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" colspan="1" align="left" scope="row" rowspan="1">Ablation therapies (e.g., laser treatment, radiofrequency ablation, electrosurgery, sclerotherapy)</td>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_5_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Consider if epistaxis does not respond to topical moisturizing therapies.</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_5_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" colspan="1" align="left" scope="row" rowspan="1">Septodermoplasty &#x00026; Young's nasal closure</td>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_5_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Consider if epistaxis does not respond to topical moisturizing therapies, tranexamic acid (see <xref ref-type="table" rid="hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_4">Table 5</xref>), ablative therapies, &#x00026;/or systemic antiangiogenics (see <xref ref-type="table" rid="hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_4">Table 5</xref>). Surgical treatment for severe epistaxis in persons w/HHT should be performed by surgeons who regularly treat people w/HHT.&#x000a0;<sup>1</sup></td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_5_1_1_1_1" rowspan="3" valign="middle" align="left" scope="rowgroup" colspan="1">
<bold>GI bleeding</bold>
</td>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_5_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>EGD is 1st-line diagnostic approach for suspected bleeding.&#x000a0;<sup>2</sup></p></list-item><list-item><p>Consider capsule endoscopy if EGD does not identify the source of suspected GI bleeding to help localize the source of bleeding.&#x000a0;<sup>3</sup></p></list-item></list>
</td>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_5_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Endoscopic argon plasma coagulation may be administered concurrent w/initial EGD but should be used sparingly during endoscopy &#x00026; frequent repeat sessions should be avoided.&#x000a0;<sup>2</sup></td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_5_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" colspan="1" align="left" scope="row" rowspan="1">Oral antifibrinolytics (e.g., tranexamic acid; see <xref ref-type="table" rid="hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_4">Table 5</xref>).</td>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_5_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Treatment is typically unnecessary unless iron therapy has been ineffective in maintaining a normal Hgb level.</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_5_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" colspan="1" align="left" scope="row" rowspan="1">Systemic antiangiogenics (e.g., bevacizumab; see <xref ref-type="table" rid="hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_4">Table 5</xref>)</td>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_5_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">To consider in those w/GI bleeding that requires frequent IV iron therapy or blood transfusions&#x000a0;<sup>2</sup></td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_5_1_1_1_1" valign="middle" align="left" scope="row" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Anemia</bold>
</td>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_5_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Oral iron therapy</p></list-item><list-item><p>IV iron in those intolerant or unresponsive to oral iron therapy</p></list-item><list-item><p>RBC transfusion only in those w/hemodynamic instability/shock, comorbidities that require a higher Hgb target, acute need to increase Hgb (e.g., prior to surgery or pregnancy), or inability to maintain adequate Hgb &#x0003e;7-8 g/dL despite frequent iron infusions</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_5_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Optimal assessment of anemia &#x00026; hematologic support can be found in consensus statement on terminology&#x000a0;<sup>4</sup></td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_5_1_1_1_1" rowspan="3" valign="middle" align="left" scope="rowgroup" colspan="1">
<bold>Pulmonary AVMs</bold>
</td>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_5_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Transcatheter embolotherapy is treatment of choice &#x00026; should be considered for any pulmonary AVM w/feeding artery &#x0003e;2-3 mm in diameter.&#x000a0;<sup>5</sup></td>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_5_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Treatment is additionally indicated for pulmonary AVM assoc w/dyspnea, exercise intolerance, &#x00026; hypoxemia, but is most important for prevention of lung hemorrhage &#x00026; neurologic complications of brain abscess &#x00026; stroke, even in those w/normal pulmonary function &#x00026; oxygen saturation.</p></list-item><list-item><p>See footnote 6.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Occasionally, a small lesion cannot be reached by transcatheter embolotherapy because of its location or the size of the feeding vessel.</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_5_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" colspan="1" align="left" scope="row" rowspan="1">Chest CT &#x00026;/or contrast echocardiography after embolization of pulmonary AVMs because of reported recanalization &#x00026; development or growth of untreated pulmonary AVMs&#x000a0;<sup>7</sup></td>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_5_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Usually, follow-up CT 6-12 mos post occlusion; then, if no recanalized or new pulmonary AVMs are noted, follow-up CT every 3 yrs&#x000a0;<sup>5</sup></td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_5_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" colspan="1" align="left" scope="row" rowspan="1">If contrast echocardiography shows pulmonary shunting (even if no pulmonary AVM is identified on chest CT):
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Prophylactic antibiotics in accordance w/American Heart Association protocol for dental cleaning &#x00026; other procedures w/risk of bacteremia is advised because of risk of abscess (esp brain abscess) assoc w/right-to-left shunting.&#x000a0;<sup>8</sup></p></list-item><list-item><p>Caution not to introduce air bubbles (incl air filter if available) is recommended w/IV lines.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Avoid scuba diving in those w/evidence of pulmonary AVMs.</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_5_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Note: The risk assoc w/these lesions is not for subacute bacterial endocarditis.</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_5_1_1_1_1" rowspan="2" valign="middle" align="left" scope="rowgroup" colspan="1">
<bold>Hepatic AVMs</bold>
</td>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_5_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Most w/symptomatic hepatic AVMs can be managed w/intensive medical therapy.&#x000a0;<sup>9</sup></p></list-item><list-item><p>Consider IV bevacizumab (see <xref ref-type="table" rid="hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_4">Table 5</xref>) for those w/symptomatic high-output cardiac failure due to hepatic AVMs who have failed to respond to 1st-line mgmt.&#x000a0;<sup>2</sup></p></list-item></list>
</td>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_5_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>See footnote 6.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Treatment of symptomatic hepatic AVMs is currently problematic. Embolization of hepatic AVMs (successful for treatment of pulmonary AVMs) has led to lethal hepatic infarctions.</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_5_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" colspan="1" align="left" scope="row" rowspan="1">Referral for consideration of liver transplantation for those w/symptomatic hepatic AVMs causing refractory high-output heart failure, biliary ischemia, or complicated portal hypertension.&#x000a0;<sup>2</sup></td>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_5_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Liver transplantation has been standard treatment for those (usually older) persons whose symptoms of hepatic failure do not respond to medical mgmt.&#x000a0;<sup>10</sup></p></list-item><list-item><p>Liver biopsy should be avoided in persons w/HHT.&#x000a0;<sup>11</sup></p></list-item></list>
</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_5_1_1_1_1" valign="middle" align="left" scope="row" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Cerebral AVMs</bold>
</td>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_5_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Cerebral AVMs are typically treated using neurovascular surgery, embolotherapy, &#x00026;/or stereotactic radiosurgery depending on size, location, &#x00026; symptoms.</td>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_5_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Recurrence can be seen after successful treatment, esp in children, so follow up w/MRI &#x00026; cerebral angiography is recommended.&#x000a0;<sup>6,&#x000a0;12</sup></td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_5_1_1_1_1" valign="middle" align="left" scope="row" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Pulmonary artery</bold>
<break/>
<bold>hypertension</bold>
</td>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_5_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Treatment per cardiologist, pulmonologist, &#x00026; other relevant specialists</td>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_5_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_5_1_1_1_1" valign="middle" align="left" scope="row" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Intestinal polyps assoc w/<italic toggle="yes">SMAD4</italic>-related HHT</bold>
</td>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_5_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">See Juvenile Polyposis Syndrome, <related-object source-id="gene" document-id="jps" object-id="jps.Treatment_of_Manifestations">Treatment of Manifestations</related-object>.</td>
                    <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_5_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
              <table-wrap-foot>
                <fn>
                  <p>AVMs = arteriovenous malformations; EGD = esophagogastroduodenoscopy; GI = gastrointestinal; Hgb = hemoglobin; HHT = hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia; IV = intravenous; RBC = red blood cell</p>
                </fn>
                <fn id="hht.TF.6.1">
                  <label>1. </label>
                  <p>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.richer.2012.401">Richer et al [2012]</xref>
</p>
                </fn>
                <fn id="hht.TF.6.2">
                  <label>2. </label>
                  <p>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.faughnan.2020.989">Faughnan et al [2020]</xref>
</p>
                </fn>
                <fn id="hht.TF.6.3">
                  <label>3. </label>
                  <p>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.gr_ve.2010.760">Gr&#x000e8;ve et al [2010]</xref>
</p>
                </fn>
                <fn id="hht.TF.6.4">
                  <label>4. </label>
                  <p>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.alsamkari.2025b.1813">Al-Samkari et al [2025b]</xref>
</p>
                </fn>
                <fn id="hht.TF.6.5">
                  <label>5. </label>
                  <p>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.faughnan.2009.1186">Faughnan et al [2009]</xref>
</p>
                </fn>
                <fn id="hht.TF.6.6">
                  <label>6. </label>
                  <p>Before proceeding with treatment for any visceral AVM, affected individuals and their doctors are encouraged to contact the nearest multidisciplinary HHT clinic, which can be located through the support group <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://curehht.org/">Cure HHT</ext-link>, to ensure that appropriate diagnostic and treatment plans are in place [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.faughnan.2011.73">Faughnan et al 2011</xref>].</p>
                </fn>
                <fn id="hht.TF.6.7">
                  <label>7. </label>
                  <p>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.cottin.2007.361">Cottin et al [2007]</xref>
</p>
                </fn>
                <fn id="hht.TF.6.8">
                  <label>8. </label>
                  <p>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.dupuisgirod.2017.315">Dupuis-Girod et al [2017]</xref>
</p>
                </fn>
                <fn id="hht.TF.6.9">
                  <label>9. </label>
                  <p>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.buscarini.2011.2166">Buscarini et al [2011]</xref>
</p>
                </fn>
                <fn id="hht.TF.6.10">
                  <label>10. </label>
                  <p>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.iyer.2019.1418">Iyer et al [2019]</xref>
</p>
                </fn>
                <fn id="hht.TF.6.11">
                  <label>11. </label>
                  <p>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.buscarini.2006.1040">Buscarini et al [2006]</xref>
</p>
                </fn>
                <fn id="hht.TF.6.12">
                  <label>12. </label>
                  <p>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.oulasvirta.2023.1565">Oulasvirta et al [2023]</xref>
</p>
                </fn>
              </table-wrap-foot>
            </table-wrap>
          </sec>
        </sec>
        <sec id="hht.Surveillance">
          <title>Surveillance</title>
          <p>The evaluations summarized in <xref ref-type="table" rid="hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_6">Table 7</xref> are recommended for all individuals with an established diagnosis of HHT and for all individuals at risk for HHT based on family history in whom HHT has not been ruled out by molecular genetic testing [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.faughnan.2020.989">Faughnan et al 2020</xref>].</p>
          <table-wrap id="hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_6" orientation="portrait" position="anchor">
            <label>Table 7. </label>
            <caption>
              <p>Hereditary Hemorrhagic Telangiectasia: Recommended Surveillance</p>
            </caption>
            <table frame="hsides" rules="groups">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th id="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_6_1_1_1_1" valign="middle" align="left" scope="col" rowspan="1" colspan="1">System/Concern</th>
                  <th id="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_6_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" align="left" scope="col" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Evaluation</th>
                  <th id="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_6_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" scope="col" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Frequency</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_6_1_1_1_1" valign="middle" align="left" scope="row" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>General</bold>
</td>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_6_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Eval by health care provider familiar w/HHT, incl interval history for epistaxis, other bleeding, shortness of breath, reduced exercise tolerance, headache, &#x00026; other neurologic symptoms</td>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_6_1_1_1_3" rowspan="2" valign="middle" align="left" colspan="1">Annually</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_6_1_1_1_1" valign="middle" align="left" scope="row" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Anemia</bold>
</td>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_6_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Hematocrit/Hgb</p></list-item><list-item><p>Ferritin</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_6_1_1_1_1" valign="middle" align="left" scope="row" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Pulmonary AVMs</bold>
</td>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_6_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>In <bold>adults</bold>: TCE w/agitated saline contrast if previous TCE did not reveal evidence of significant right-to-left shunt</p></list-item><list-item><p>In <bold>children</bold>: TCE w/agitated saline contrast OR chest radiograph w/pulse oximetry&#x000a0;<sup>1</sup></p></list-item></list>
</td>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_6_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>TCE should generally be done every 5 yrs in those w/normal TCE.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Some HHT centers recommend continued surveillance w/TCE for those w/mild shunt&#x000a0;<sup>2</sup> &#x00026; proceed to chest CT if shunt worsens to moderate or severe. Other centers perform chest CT in all persons w/abnormal findings on TCE (even mild shunt), &#x00026; then monitor by chest CT every 5 yrs.</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_6_1_1_1_1" valign="middle" align="left" scope="row" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Cerebral AVMs</bold>
</td>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_6_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Brain MRI (w/ &#x00026; w/o contrast using sequences that detect blood products)</td>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_6_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Repeat MRI by age 18-20 yrs is routinely done in the US&#x000a0;<sup>3</sup> if initial MRI was done in childhood, as development or evolution of cerebral AVMs in 1st 2 decades of life has been reported.&#x000a0;<sup>4</sup></p></list-item><list-item><p>Some centers repeat MRI every 5-10 yrs prior to adulthood.</p></list-item><list-item><p>If MRI is normal by age 18-20 yrs, repeat MRI is generally not recommended.</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_6_1_1_1_1" valign="middle" align="left" scope="row" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Hepatic AVM</bold>
</td>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_6_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>History &#x00026; exam for manifestations of heart failure or liver failure.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Options in adults include Doppler ultrasound (best option when local expertise is available), multiphase CT angiography, &#x00026; contrast abdominal MRI.</p></list-item><list-item><p>In individuals w/significant hepatic AVM or symptoms of heart failure, echocardiography w/measurement of cardiac output is warranted.</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_6_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">The latest guidelines recommend imaging in adults to screen for hepatic AVMs.&#x000a0;<sup>1</sup> Although they are usually asymptomatic, it is felt that early detection of hepatic AVMs will allow for better prognostication &#x00026; follow up. The optimal age to start screening is unknown.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_6_1_1_1_1" valign="middle" align="left" scope="row" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold><italic toggle="yes">SMAD4</italic>-related HHT</bold>
</td>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_6_1_1_1_2" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">See Juvenile Polyposis Syndrome, <related-object source-id="gene" document-id="jps" object-id="jps.Surveillance">Surveillance</related-object> for additional recommendations.</td>
                  <td headers="hd_h_hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_6_1_1_1_3" valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Colonoscopy at age 15 yrs; repeat every 3 yrs if no polyps found or annually w/EGD if colonic polyps are identified.&#x000a0;<sup>1</sup></td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
            <table-wrap-foot>
              <fn>
                <p>AVM = arteriovenous malformation; EGD = esophagogastroduodenoscopy; Hgb = hemoglobin; HHT = hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia; TCE = transthoracic contrast echocardiography; US = United States</p>
              </fn>
              <fn id="hht.TF.7.1">
                <label>1. </label>
                <p>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.faughnan.2020.989">Faughnan et al [2020]</xref>
</p>
              </fn>
              <fn id="hht.TF.7.2">
                <label>2. </label>
                <p>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.velthuis.2015.255">Velthuis et al [2015]</xref>
</p>
              </fn>
              <fn id="hht.TF.7.3">
                <label>3. </label>
                <p>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.beslow.2024.421">Beslow et al [2024]</xref>
</p>
              </fn>
              <fn id="hht.TF.7.4">
                <label>4. </label>
                <p>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.hetts.2014.1376">Hetts et al [2014]</xref>
</p>
              </fn>
            </table-wrap-foot>
          </table-wrap>
        </sec>
        <sec id="hht.AgentsCircumstances_to_Avoid">
          <title>Agents/Circumstances to Avoid</title>
          <p>Individuals with significant epistaxis are advised to avoid vigorous nose blowing, lifting of heavy objects, straining during bowel movements, and finger manipulation in the nose. Some individuals with HHT experience increased epistaxis after drinking alcohol.</p>
          <p>Anticoagulants including aspirin and nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory agents such as ibuprofen that interfere with normal clotting should be avoided unless required for treatment of other medical conditions. However, HHT is not an absolute contraindication for anticoagulation (prophylactic or therapeutic) or antiplatelet therapy. It is recommended that individuals with HHT receive these therapies when there is an indication, with consideration of their individualized bleeding risks [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.faughnan.2020.989">Faughnan et al 2020</xref>]. Two case series demonstrated that anticoagulation or antiplatelet therapy is well tolerated by most individuals with HHT [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.edwards.2012.1959">Edwards et al 2012</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.devlin.2013.876">Devlin et al 2013</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.shovlin.2019.210">Shovlin et al 2019</xref>]. Note: A nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug that does not inhibit platelet aggregation may be preferred (e.g., celecoxib).</p>
          <p>When anticoagulation is pursued, unfractionated heparin, low-molecular-weight heparin, and vitamin K antagonists are preferred over direct-acting oral anticoagulants, which are less well tolerated in individuals with HHT [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.shovlin.2019.210">Shovlin et al 2019</xref>]. In individuals with atrial fibrillation, if anticoagulation is not tolerated, alternate approaches can be considered, such as left atrial appendage closure [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.vorselaars.2015.49">Vorselaars et al 2015</xref>]. Avoiding the use of dual antiplatelet therapy and/or combination of antiplatelet and anticoagulation is recommended when possible.</p>
          <p>Scuba diving should be avoided unless contrast echocardiography performed within the last five years was negative for evidence of a right-to-left shunt.</p>
          <p>Liver biopsy should be avoided in individuals with HHT especially if they have a documented liver arteriovenous malformation (AVM) [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.buscarini.2006.1040">Buscarini et al 2006</xref>].</p>
        </sec>
        <sec id="hht.Evaluation_of_Relatives_at_Risk">
          <title>Evaluation of Relatives at Risk</title>
          <p>Evaluation of apparently asymptomatic older and younger at-risk relatives of an affected individual is recommended in order to identify as early as possible those who would benefit from <xref ref-type="sec" rid="hht.Surveillance">surveillance</xref> and prompt initiation of <xref ref-type="sec" rid="hht.Treatment_of_Manifestations">treatment</xref> to reduce morbidity and mortality. Evaluations can include the following:</p>
          <list list-type="bullet">
            <list-item>
              <p>If the HHT-related pathogenic variant in the family is known, molecular genetic testing can be performed.</p>
            </list-item>
            <list-item>
              <p>If the familial pathogenic variant is not known and the diagnosis of HHT cannot been ruled out by molecular testing, at-risk family members should follow the same protocol as is recommended for individuals in whom the diagnosis of HHT is established (see <xref ref-type="sec" rid="hht.Surveillance">Surveillance</xref>).</p>
              <list list-type="bullet">
                <list-item>
                  <p>Individuals older than age 40 years should have a targeted medical history and clinical examination for features of HHT. The absence of even mild but recurrent epistaxis and subtle telangiectases in characteristic locations on careful examination is reassuring.</p>
                </list-item>
                <list-item>
                  <p>In individuals age 40 years and younger, targeted medical history and clinical examination for features of HHT in addition to an evaluation for brain and pulmonary AVMs should be done initially, as features of HHT may not be identified by medical history and clinical examination in younger individuals.</p>
                </list-item>
              </list>
            </list-item>
          </list>
          <p>See <xref ref-type="sec" rid="hht.Related_Genetic_Counseling_Issues">Genetic Counseling</xref> for issues related to testing of at-risk relatives for genetic counseling purposes.</p>
        </sec>
        <sec id="hht.Pregnancy_Management">
          <title>Pregnancy Management</title>
          <p>Pregnant women with HHT and untreated pulmonary AVMs are at increased risk for complications related to hemorrhage or embolic events. Women with treated pulmonary AVMs appear to be at no higher risk during pregnancy than those without pulmonary AVMs. For this reason, published management guidelines recommend that pregnant women with HHT who have not been recently screened and/or treated for pulmonary AVMs should be managed as follows [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.dupuis.2020.5">Dupuis et al 2020</xref>]:</p>
          <list list-type="bullet">
            <list-item>
              <p>In asymptomatic women, initial screening for pulmonary AVMs should be performed using either transthoracic contrast echocardiography with agitated saline contrast or low-dose non-contrast chest CT, depending on local expertise. Chest CT, when performed, should be done early in the second trimester.</p>
            </list-item>
            <list-item>
              <p>In women with clinical manifestations suggestive of a pulmonary AVM, diagnostic testing should be performed using low-dose non-contrast chest CT. This testing can be performed at any gestational age, as clinically indicated.</p>
            </list-item>
            <list-item>
              <p>Pulmonary AVMs should be treated starting in the second trimester unless otherwise clinically indicated.</p>
            </list-item>
          </list>
          <p>An unenhanced brain MRI is recommended in pregnant women with clinical manifestations suggestive of a cerebral AVM. Women who are asymptomatic do not require routine screening for cerebral AVMs during pregnancy.</p>
          <p>Iron replacement is preferred for anemia, but transfusion of packed red blood cells may be necessary for symptomatic anemia refractory to aggressive iron replacement therapy.</p>
          <p>Expert consensus is to not withhold an epidural because of a diagnosis of HHT, and that screening for spinal vascular malformations is not required. Two case series showed no evidence of hemorrhagic complications from epidural or spinal anesthesia [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.de_gussem.2014.514">de Gussem et al 2014</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.dupuis.2020.5">Dupuis et al 2020</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.faughnan.2020.989">Faughnan et al 2020</xref>].</p>
        </sec>
        <sec id="hht.Therapies_Under_Investigation">
          <title>Therapies Under Investigation</title>
          <p>Newer therapies designed to interfere with the development of abnormal vascular connections are being investigated; while numerous case reports, small uncontrolled series, and small randomized clinical trials indicate promise, results from larger controlled trials are needed. Specific agents under various stages of study include the following:</p>
          <list list-type="bullet">
            <list-item>
              <p>ALN-6400, an investigational RNA interference agent designed to lower plasminogen levels and act as a potential hemostatic agent</p>
            </list-item>
            <list-item>
              <p>Nintedanib, a tyrosine kinase inhibitor that blocks receptors for various growth factors including VEGF [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.hermann.2024.9">Hermann et al 2024</xref>]</p>
            </list-item>
            <list-item>
              <p>Tacrolimus, which binds to intracellular FKBP12, which ultimately activates the downstream Smad1/5/8 signaling cascade [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.hessels.2022.5280">Hessels et al 2022</xref>]</p>
            </list-item>
            <list-item>
              <p>Sirolimus, which binds to intracellular FKBP12, and the resulting complex directly inhibits mTOR</p>
            </list-item>
            <list-item>
              <p>VAD044 (engasertib), an investigational AKT pathway inhibitor [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.alsamkari.2025a.2131">Al-Samkari et al 2025a</xref>]</p>
            </list-item>
          </list>
          <p>Search <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://clinicaltrials.gov">ClinicalTrials.gov</ext-link> in the US and <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.clinicaltrialsregister.eu/ctr-search/search">EU Clinical Trials Register</ext-link> in Europe for access to information on clinical studies for a wide range of diseases and conditions.</p>
        </sec>
      </sec>
      <sec id="hht.Genetic_Counseling">
        <title>Genetic Counseling</title>
        <p content-type="genetic_counseling">
<italic toggle="yes">Genetic counseling is the process of providing individuals and families with
information on the nature, mode(s) of inheritance, and implications of genetic disorders to help them
make informed medical and personal decisions. The following section deals with genetic
risk assessment and the use of family history and genetic testing to clarify genetic
status for family members; it is not meant to address all personal, cultural, or
ethical issues that may arise or to substitute for consultation with a genetics
professional</italic>. &#x02014;ED.</p>
        <sec id="hht.Mode_of_Inheritance">
          <title>Mode of Inheritance</title>
          <p>Hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia (HHT) is inherited in an autosomal dominant manner.</p>
        </sec>
        <sec id="hht.Risk_to_Family_Members">
          <title>Risk to Family Members</title>
          <p>
<bold>Parents of a proband</bold>
</p>
          <list list-type="bullet">
            <list-item>
              <p>Most individuals diagnosed with HHT have an affected parent.</p>
            </list-item>
            <list-item>
              <p>Rarely, a proband with HHT has the disorder as the result of a <italic toggle="yes">de novo</italic> germline or postzygotic mosaic pathogenic variant [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.best.2011.358">Best et al 2011</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.mcdonald.2015.1">McDonald et al 2015</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.mcdonald.2018.1618">McDonald et al 2018</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.t_rring.2018.121">T&#x000f8;rring et al 2018</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.clarke.2020.859">Clarke et al 2020</xref>].</p>
            </list-item>
            <list-item>
              <p>If a molecular diagnosis has been established in the proband and the proband appears to be the only affected family member (i.e., a simplex case), molecular genetic testing is recommended for the parents of the proband to evaluate their genetic status, inform recurrence risk assessment, and determine if a parent should undergo clinical examination for features of HHT.</p>
            </list-item>
            <list-item>
              <p>If the HHT-related pathogenic variant has not been identified in the proband, recommendations for the evaluation of parents of the proband include physical examination and documentation of medical history targeted at symptoms and manifestations of HHT. Note: If the familial pathogenic variant is not known and, consequently, HHT cannot been ruled out by molecular genetic testing, at-risk family members should follow the same protocol as is recommended for individuals in whom the diagnosis of HHT is definite (see <xref ref-type="sec" rid="hht.Surveillance">Surveillance</xref>).</p>
            </list-item>
            <list-item>
              <p>If the proband has a known HHT-related pathogenic variant that cannot be detected in leukocyte DNA of either parent and parental identity testing has confirmed biological maternity and paternity, the following possibilities should be considered:</p>
              <list list-type="bullet">
                <list-item>
                  <p>The proband has a <italic toggle="yes">de novo</italic> pathogenic variant.</p>
                </list-item>
                <list-item>
                  <p>The proband inherited a pathogenic variant from a parent with gonadal (or somatic and gonadal) mosaicism [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.mcdonald.2018.1618">McDonald et al 2018</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.t_rring.2018.121">T&#x000f8;rring et al 2018</xref>]. Note: Testing of parental leukocyte DNA may not detect all instances of somatic mosaicism and will not detect a pathogenic variant that is present in the germ (gonadal) cells only.</p>
                </list-item>
              </list>
            </list-item>
            <list-item>
              <p>A proband may appear to be the only affected family member because of failure to recognize the disorder in affected family members, reduced penetrance, early death of the parent before the onset of symptoms, or late onset of the disease in the affected parent. Therefore, <italic toggle="yes">de novo</italic> occurrence of an HHT-related pathogenic variant cannot be confirmed unless molecular genetic testing has demonstrated that neither parent is heterozygous for HHT-related pathogenic variant.</p>
            </list-item>
          </list>
          <p><bold>Sibs of a proband.</bold> The risk to the sibs depends on the genetic status of the proband's parents:</p>
          <list list-type="bullet">
            <list-item>
              <p>If a parent of the proband is affected and/or is known to have the HHT-related pathogenic variant identified in the proband, the risk to the sibs of inheriting the pathogenic variant is 50%.</p>
              <list list-type="bullet">
                <list-item>
                  <p>HHT exhibits considerable intrafamilial variability and age-related penetrance, which is manifestation dependent.</p>
                </list-item>
                <list-item>
                  <p>Penetrance of HHT approaches 100% by late adulthood.</p>
                </list-item>
              </list>
            </list-item>
            <list-item>
              <p>If the proband has a known HHT-causing pathogenic variant that cannot be detected in the leukocyte DNA of either parent, the recurrence risk to sibs is slightly greater than that of the general population because of the possibility of parental mosaicism [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.mcdonald.2018.1618">McDonald et al 2018</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.t_rring.2018.121">T&#x000f8;rring et al 2018</xref>].</p>
            </list-item>
            <list-item>
              <p>If the parents are clinically unaffected but their genetic status is unknown, sibs should be considered at risk for HHT and, if the familial pathogenic variant is known, offered molecular genetic testing. If the familial pathogenic variant is not known, sibs should follow the same protocol as is recommended for individuals in whom the diagnosis of HHT is definite (see <xref ref-type="sec" rid="hht.Surveillance">Surveillance</xref>). Although HHT is highly penetrant, a heterozygous parent may not have manifestations of the disorder until after age 40 years and observable symptoms and manifestations can be subtle even well into adulthood.</p>
            </list-item>
          </list>
          <p><bold>Offspring of a proband.</bold> Each child of an individual with HHT has a 50% chance of inheriting the pathogenic variant.</p>
          <p><bold>Other family members.</bold> The risk to other family members depends on the status of the proband's parents: if a parent is affected and/or has an HHT-related pathogenic variant, the parent's family members are at risk.</p>
        </sec>
        <sec id="hht.Related_Genetic_Counseling_Issues">
          <title>Related Genetic Counseling Issues</title>
          <p>See Management, <xref ref-type="sec" rid="hht.Evaluation_of_Relatives_at_Risk">Evaluation of Relatives at Risk</xref> for information on evaluating at-risk relatives for the purpose of early diagnosis and treatment.</p>
          <p>
<bold>Predictive testing (i.e., testing of asymptomatic at-risk individuals)</bold>
</p>
          <list list-type="bullet">
            <list-item>
              <p>Predictive testing for at-risk relatives is possible once the HHT-related pathogenic variant has been identified in an affected family member.</p>
            </list-item>
            <list-item>
              <p>Consideration of molecular genetic testing of young at-risk family members is appropriate for guiding medical management (see <xref ref-type="sec" rid="hht.Surveillance">Surveillance</xref>).</p>
            </list-item>
            <list-item>
              <p>Potential consequences of such testing (including, but not limited to, socioeconomic changes and the need for long-term follow up and evaluation arrangements for individuals with a positive test result) as well as the capabilities and limitations of predictive testing should be discussed in the context of formal genetic counseling prior to testing.</p>
            </list-item>
          </list>
          <p>
<bold>Family planning</bold>
</p>
          <list list-type="bullet">
            <list-item>
              <p>The optimal time for determination of genetic risk and discussion of the availability of prenatal/preimplantation genetic testing is before pregnancy.</p>
            </list-item>
            <list-item>
              <p>It is appropriate to offer genetic counseling (including discussion of potential risks to offspring and reproductive options) to young adults who are affected or at risk.</p>
            </list-item>
          </list>
          <p><bold>DNA banking.</bold> Because it is likely that testing methodology and our understanding of genes, pathogenic mechanisms, and diseases will improve in the future, consideration should be given to banking DNA from probands in whom a molecular diagnosis has not been confirmed (i.e., the causative pathogenic mechanism is unknown). For more information, see <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.huang.2022.389">Huang et al [2022]</xref>.</p>
        </sec>
        <sec id="hht.Prenatal_Testing_and_Preimplantation">
          <title>Prenatal Testing and Preimplantation Genetic Testing</title>
          <p>Once the HHT-related pathogenic variant has been identified in an affected family member, prenatal and preimplantation genetic testing are possible.</p>
          <p>Differences in perspective may exist among medical professionals and within families regarding the use of prenatal and preimplantation genetic testing. While most health care professionals would consider use of prenatal and preimplantation genetic testing to be a personal decision, discussion of these issues may be helpful.</p>
        </sec>
      </sec>
      <sec id="hht.Resources">
        <title>Resources</title>
        <p content-type="resources">
<italic toggle="yes">GeneReviews staff has selected the following disease-specific and/or umbrella
support organizations and/or registries for the benefit of individuals with this disorder
and their families. GeneReviews is not responsible for the information provided by other
organizations. For information on selection criteria, click <related-object source-id="gene" document-id="app4">here</related-object>.</italic></p>
        <p specific-use="from-external-xml">
          <list list-type="bullet" list-content="resource-list">
            <list-item>
              <p>
                <bold>Cure HHT</bold>
              </p>
              <p><bold>Phone:</bold> 410-357-9932</p>
              <p><bold>Email:</bold> hhtinfo@curehht.org</p>
