        <sec id="hht.Molecular_Pathogenesis">
          <title>Molecular Pathogenesis</title>
          <p>Currently, all known genetic defects that cause hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia (HHT) are in genes that encode proteins within the transforming growth factor beta (TGF-&#x003b2;) signaling pathway.</p>
          <list list-type="bullet">
            <list-item>
              <p><italic toggle="yes">ACVRL1</italic> encodes activin receptor type-1-like (also called serine/threonine-protein kinase receptor R3), a type I cell surface receptor for the TGF superfamily of ligands. It is expressed predominantly on endothelial cells [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="hht.REF.abdalla.2006.97">Abdalla &#x00026; Letarte 2006</xref>].</p>
            </list-item>
            <list-item>
              <p><italic toggle="yes">ENG</italic> encodes endoglin, a component of the TGF-&#x003b2; receptor complex. It is expressed predominantly on endothelial cells.</p>
            </list-item>
            <list-item>
              <p><italic toggle="yes">SMAD4</italic> encodes a 552-amino acid protein that functions as an intracellular signaling molecule in the TGF-&#x003b2;/BMP pathway.</p>
            </list-item>
          </list>
          <p><bold>Mechanism of disease causation.</bold> Loss of function</p>
          <table-wrap id="hht.T.hereditary_hemorrhagic_telangiecta_7" orientation="portrait" position="anchor">
            <label>Table 8. </label>
            <caption>
              <p>Hereditary Hemorrhagic Telangiectasia: Gene-Specific Laboratory Considerations</p>
            </caption>
            <table frame="hsides" rules="groups">
              <thead>
                <tr>
