      <sec id="hht.Chapter_Notes">
        <title>Chapter Notes</title>
        <sec id="hht.Author_Notes">
          <title>Author Notes</title>
          <p><bold>David Stevenson, MD,</bold> is a practicing medical geneticist at Stanford University and helps direct the Stanford HHT Center of Excellence.<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://www.stanfordchildrens.org/en/service/genetics/vascular-anomaly-genetic-clinic">www.stanfordchildrens.org/en/service/genetics/vascular-anomaly-genetic-clinic</ext-link></p>
          <p><bold>Jamie McDonald MS, CGC,</bold> Associate Professor, Emeritus, Department of Pathology at the University of Utah was the Co-Director of the University of Utah HHT Center of Excellence from 2009 to 2021; and its co-founder and genetic counselor since 1996. Jamie has many publications related to HHT, including invited review articles and original research focused particularly on the clinical and molecular diagnosis of HHT. She has served on the Cure HHT Global Research and Medical Advisory Board for many years. She co-chaired the Clinical and Molecular Diagnosis Group at the International HHT Guidelines Conference, which led to publication of the initial consensus guidelines for diagnosis and management in 2011. She was an invited member of the international conference that led to the publication in 2021 of the second consensus guidelines for the diagnosis and management of HHT.<email xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="jamie.mcdonald@hsc.utah.edu">jamie.mcdonald@hsc.utah.edu</email></p>
          <p><bold>James R Gossage, MD,</bold> Professor of Medicine, Section of Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine, Department of Internal Medicine, Augusta University, Georgia. Dr Gossage has been involved with HHT patient care and clinical research since 1998 with an emphasis on management of pulmonary AVMs and bleeding. He was Medical Director of Cure HHT from 2008 to 2018 and has served on many HHT-related work groups and conference committees. He was an invited member of the 2009 and 2020 international consensus conferences that led to publication of HHT consensus guidelines. Dr Gossage would be happy to communicate with clinicians who have questions regarding diagnosis and treatment of HHT.<email xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="jgossage@augusta.edu">jgossage@augusta.edu</email></p>
        </sec>
        <sec id="hht.Acknowledgments">
          <title>Acknowledgments</title>
          <p>Pinar Bayrak-Toydemir, MD, PhD, Department of Pathology, University of Utah</p>
        </sec>
        <sec id="hht.Author_History">
          <title>Author History</title>
          <p>James R Gossage, MD (2026-present)Alan Guttmacher, MD; National Institutes of Health (2000-2009) Jamie McDonald, MS, CGC (2000-present) Reed E Pyeritz, MD, PhD, FACMG; Perelman School of Medicine, University of Pennsylvania (2009-2021)David Stevenson, MD (2021-present)</p>
        </sec>
